ind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141048/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141048"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-348093/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "b284742f-25d4-5bd5-8831-cd5d114f143a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348093/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ced5e725-7f07-5645-b5b2-a570c0e8eec7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-348093/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-348093/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-348093" :question/id #uuid "6f774a2d-4bc6-5fc1-ab21-b88e43fd39a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-348093" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-348093/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-348093"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-142369/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "0d0a0789-3785-53cd-addf-aafd5662c01c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142369/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "410a1425-86fd-5750-b212-210db7f027ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142369/selection/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9" :answer/id #uuid "f9fd7553-30d2-5940-ac73-c78aa49154f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142369/selection/b7e1c893089e9c7b8066a199c43cdff517fe004f1db62d878596274884ad36ec" :answer/id #uuid "1026e018-2f6c-586e-928e-e7b9871c2f9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142369/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "de127e39-67bc-52c6-b259-08809cc49a98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142369/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a78b0471-844d-5027-ad7c-a973a630a67e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-142369/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-142369/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-142369/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-142369/selection/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9" "q-142369/selection/b7e1c893089e9c7b8066a199c43cdff517fe004f1db62d878596274884ad36ec" "q-142369/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-142369" :question/id #uuid "c6d7bb79-18ae-5765-94d3-bc45d36669b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-142369" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/40375a9794d9e399327cfcf128e017f05d20319fd86d41a8a9f49e01f58ccae0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4$  (shown above) at $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =7\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-142369/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-142369"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353227/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4" :answer/id #uuid "76eb17b8-fa49-507f-bf5f-0bdc0d354522" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\cot(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353227/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "de303eef-640c-5ab4-8dca-8fdfa2e608d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353227/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4" :answer-field/choices ["q-353227/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353227" :question/id #uuid "0c0e48fc-bca3-50ea-ba63-bf42dda0d6a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353227" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=13,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=13,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-353227/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353227"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-348096/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4" :answer/id #uuid "f8f1eeec-927c-5411-8112-5df661e2b9f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\cot(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348096/field-1/eb039d5c8c53c428103f2f47e214428c8b584c2b411f79d79a9cf5217e4fb542" :answer/id #uuid "6576e1d1-d575-5ce0-90aa-317792fda81b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cot(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348096/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6392e8d-612e-5aa3-8189-40306a768542" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-348096/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4" :answer-field/choices ["q-348096/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4" "q-348096/field-1/eb039d5c8c53c428103f2f47e214428c8b584c2b411f79d79a9cf5217e4fb542"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-348096" :question/id #uuid "79eb2daf-bacf-5407-b324-69f1a44e9c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-348096" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=18,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=18,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-348096/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-348096"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-142382/selection/a05724295bc0340162295da21b8749cd336584b7c76ed9f6810ddb03e7f98d00" :answer/id #uuid "7e1095f7-176a-553f-9294-5455d8699b5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142382/selection/fb7525825931e543af024135c3c93d2bdd2635eb2f5c80160c7ed7eedf842f5a" :answer/id #uuid "06a05e03-0901-5ea7-abb2-229bb9271d51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142382/selection/e23f9ea338f175b3f5a7621b94518bec0d179d6cb526e41f6e659cfd147c4d14" :answer/id #uuid "572324bf-0344-556c-bf26-01b4d8419acd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142382/selection/d79808b1304c1856f3712e6e226ade34c7caf51f0407b91d3e2447e1f8a47743" :answer/id #uuid "39fcf485-e2a9-5066-8124-8051d46ee8ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142382/selection/c8cc0193c097b7d38e4091db7b8ea0b55b90f570b6dfeef91f6d5caca670d84e" :answer/id #uuid "aeb962bf-879c-5c7f-850f-d9d5c81923bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142382/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f8ddd1-434f-5e87-bb73-ae2671805e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-142382/selection/a05724295bc0340162295da21b8749cd336584b7c76ed9f6810ddb03e7f98d00" :answer-field/choices ["q-142382/selection/a05724295bc0340162295da21b8749cd336584b7c76ed9f6810ddb03e7f98d00" "q-142382/selection/fb7525825931e543af024135c3c93d2bdd2635eb2f5c80160c7ed7eedf842f5a" "q-142382/selection/e23f9ea338f175b3f5a7621b94518bec0d179d6cb526e41f6e659cfd147c4d14" "q-142382/selection/d79808b1304c1856f3712e6e226ade34c7caf51f0407b91d3e2447e1f8a47743" "q-142382/selection/c8cc0193c097b7d38e4091db7b8ea0b55b90f570b6dfeef91f6d5caca670d84e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-142382" :question/id #uuid "c07c1e32-2396-5134-92cc-86e29114310e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142382" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=12$ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=12,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-142382/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-142382"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353232/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "7c6c1e82-9215-59d2-82bf-e80a9fc2318b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353232/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8546a5d0-b657-54c6-8ab3-5d3ed29b47b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353232/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-353232/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353232" :question/id #uuid "b1d6b2f9-6480-5dbc-b43c-41f181d02363" :question/math-academy-id "q-353232" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/c1fe71fe51ae93fc2e78b52b1d3f1c270ab05d10c317345bf1440b13c570483f.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=30$ (shown above), evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=30,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-353232/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353232"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353244/field-1/c8362bdfcc4d0cfb073df782afd1cfe68da960b37d15b7353e42ee2f07c57025" :answer/id #uuid "a2dc5831-1a0d-597b-a2a2-110813a0b40c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin(\\theta)+\\theta\\cos(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353244/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "08451976-c5a4-5431-a984-8358a00dcef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353244/field-1/c8362bdfcc4d0cfb073df782afd1cfe68da960b37d15b7353e42ee2f07c57025" :answer-field/choices ["q-353244/field-1/c8362bdfcc4d0cfb073df782afd1cfe68da960b37d15b7353e42ee2f07c57025"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353244" :question/id #uuid "4d2594a0-4686-55bb-800b-08f075a02c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353244" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/89ca909a3e3628e83e6aa4b2a20049ca33bd0436e130017caa9266fbea259022.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353244/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353244"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353249/field-1/65745c841abf30cffb51d8a3a8810c5d1b75d0f8e1a9968d66090185639a32ee" :answer/id #uuid "10f0590c-cfda-5588-a18e-953b08785dc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)+\\cos(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353249/field-1/9dabc9c7c7068460f1fcb10bc49d0e9da160eb7a9a807b267e6d3c1b8f70b80d" :answer/id #uuid "de1f84ca-8187-572d-8cfe-0bf00da7bb3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cos(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353249/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "11dd52f2-c9b0-5f31-a205-0ec502a1fa67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353249/field-1/65745c841abf30cffb51d8a3a8810c5d1b75d0f8e1a9968d66090185639a32ee" :answer-field/choices ["q-353249/field-1/65745c841abf30cffb51d8a3a8810c5d1b75d0f8e1a9968d66090185639a32ee" "q-353249/field-1/9dabc9c7c7068460f1fcb10bc49d0e9da160eb7a9a807b267e6d3c1b8f70b80d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353249" :question/id #uuid "beb8456c-0821-585b-a5f6-e11a32c1809e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353249" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={((\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={((\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353249/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353249"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-17115"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/dfc16cf0da1332a921899dc1d3ebcdf75a59de3d1323c83f81a61c665b135ea8.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi },$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-353412/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "26ff6be4-f1ab-5df6-b5d5-bad0eb4f965d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353412/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "47984acc-d046-5e2b-bb9b-90699bb2992f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353412/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9525c902-b725-5cd5-bef4-5160c11feccf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353412/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-353412/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-353412/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353412" :question/id #uuid "2311f571-051b-59c3-846b-c55dfbfb028e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353412" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta },$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nOur strategy will be to compute ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =0,$ and then compute their ratio.\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r={e}^{\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ), \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $\\theta =0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $\\theta =0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(0)}{{x}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-353412/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353412"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141050/selection/3d6a32a77d3369c379e21b50826fa6166f631f964f51a94e060a0aa1b66361fb" :answer/id #uuid "27d8a01d-fe66-59c5-a298-9a6e482e6f98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3\\theta }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141050/selection/37e4e5eb666acbe45d7c5d275f0a489edabef3983e0c2daaea6a1a2c76de4308" :answer/id #uuid "2a06522a-c63d-593c-b997-6387b80f303a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141050/selection/47d310083a20ea106c23178a3c2f5f2baedc00250afbc2b72dc32ceffd41df50" :answer/id #uuid "d1f330fe-2ce0-5db4-bda5-a064f9255a2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141050/selection/fb39bf8944edcec3d15a20fc548028ffd9e5e8a2885f8bd93081f73c376e6886" :answer/id #uuid "a321c264-fb59-5bbc-ac16-4a6a43a775f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141050/selection/c058ee1b5c5cb7ceb4ceef1e77115fa99146be109fdd6bf5feaa8428ffeae066" :answer/id #uuid "2bb17bb8-f961-5716-a380-0eb75b68ff41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141050/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9318495e-67a0-566c-a041-c71cd2486668" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141050/selection/fb39bf8944edcec3d15a20fc548028ffd9e5e8a2885f8bd93081f73c376e6886" :answer-field/choices ["q-141050/selection/3d6a32a77d3369c379e21b50826fa6166f631f964f51a94e060a0aa1b66361fb" "q-141050/selection/37e4e5eb666acbe45d7c5d275f0a489edabef3983e0c2daaea6a1a2c76de4308" "q-141050/selection/47d310083a20ea106c23178a3c2f5f2baedc00250afbc2b72dc32ceffd41df50" "q-141050/selection/fb39bf8944edcec3d15a20fc548028ffd9e5e8a2885f8bd93081f73c376e6886" "q-141050/selection/c058ee1b5c5cb7ceb4ceef1e77115fa99146be109fdd6bf5feaa8428ffeae066"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141050" :question/id #uuid "deae29cc-71dd-5ba2-8057-aa58580459cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-141050" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/ed90c6464c78010f38c764a677bddd3c7da22920fe4db62769e02d05934c9d24.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r={e}^{-3\\theta },$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r={e}^{-3\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3\\theta }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\theta }(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{-{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141050/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141050"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9948"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/a0eb20697f72b1ec5f06cc1ebfa4ba364c1023c915950b64cfab2367964db928.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta) .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9949"] :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta  - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10034"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/c86e5a3dd066efc462635310ff80973f468d04bad9206f410a74941ccf39f2f3.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2$  (shown above) at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-628"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/19b420204caf7f2706b4d355b5538dc347b5377d2b9f99297dbf6accc3220f59.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\theta,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\nx = \\theta\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=\\theta\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
